<title>PHILIPPINES: Manila softens stand on coconut row with Brazil.</title>
<headline>Manila softens stand on coconut row with Brazil.</headline>
<dateline>MANILA 1997-06-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Manila has decided not to go to court over a dispute with Brazil which has imposed a 121 percent import duty on Philippine dessicated coconut, a senior Philippine official said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Philippine Agriculture Secretary Salvador Escudero told reporters the dispute between the two countries could be solved with &quot;cooler heads.&quot;</p>
<p>Manila had threatened to contest in court the high tariff imposed on Philippine dessicated coconut by Brazil.</p>
<p>It said Brazil raised import duties on Philippine dessicated coconut in August 1995 to 121 percent on suspicion the commodity was subsidised by the Philippine government.</p>
<p>Dessicated coconut is shredded and dehydrated coconut meat used in confectionery, pastry and bakery products.</p>
<p>--Manila newsroom (63 2) 841-8936, Fax 817-6267</p>
